  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nUpper low over north-central Texas continues to progress eastward,\nleaving New Mexico and west Texas under a very positively-tilted\nridge. Skies have cleared quickly this evening, leaving the region\nin a light wind/clear sky weather pattern favorable for patchy\nfog. An area of fog/low stratus has already formed over the upper\nMimbres River in Grant County, as well as portions of the Otero\nMesa. Wouldn't be surprised to see this spread in coverage through\nthe overnight hours into Tuesday morning, affecting much of the\nTularosa Basin, Rio Grande Valley, and Southwest New Mexico\ndeserts. Will be something to monitor through the night. \n\nTuesday looks sunny and mild with very light southwest flow. High\npressure aloft is currently fixed across all of the Southwestern \nU.S, and looks to stay that way through much of the week ahead. \nThis will lead to dry weather, mostly light winds, and typical \nmid-November temperatures with lowland highs in the upper \n60s/lower 70s. Not much to talk about weather-wise over the next \nfew days.\n\nUpper ridge finally progresses across the Southern Rockies by next\nFriday. Both global models depict a fast-moving shortwave swing\nacross the International Border Friday/Saturday. This would be our\nnext precipitation chance. Current forecast shows this to be a\nchance of rain showers next Saturday, with generally light\namounts. Sunday into early next week looks dry following this \npassage.",
